    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-1",
      "sourceStart": 480,
      "sourceEnd": 531,
      "original": "### 1.1. Linear maps and their algebraic operations",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0003",
      "sourceStart": 531,
      "sourceEnd": 975,
      "sha256": "3fd0957a80342c2cd808f20b4d7994f68098c92f059d608d9bfaeb4ddaa58918"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 975,
      "sourceEnd": 1016,
      "original": "### 1.2. Kernel, range, nullity, and rank",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0004",
      "sourceStart": 1016,
      "sourceEnd": 1236,
      "sha256": "646955ee6913e3863e47598922a4861528579d89742d4b64a477af79c68cdf63"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-3",
      "sourceStart": 1236,
      "sourceEnd": 1470,
      "original": "### 1.3. Let $V,W$ be finite-dimensional vector spaces with bases $\\mathscr{B}=\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_n\\}, \\mathscr{C}=\\{w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\}$, respectively. The matrix representation of a linear map $T\\in \\mathcal{L}(V,W)$ is defined as follows:",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0005",
      "sourceStart": 1470,
      "sourceEnd": 1878,
      "sha256": "dc7a6a0b2c4120d1d5cbe731b48c77cad388d3707e6b9ac5b86857fd33707f00"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-4",
      "sourceStart": 1878,
      "sourceEnd": 1998,
      "original": "### 1.4. Let $m,n$ be positive integers. Then $F^{m\\times n}$ is the set of all $m$-by-$n$ matrices with entries in $F$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0006",
      "sourceStart": 1998,
      "sourceEnd": 2000,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-5",
      "sourceStart": 2000,
      "sourceEnd": 2084,
      "original": "### 1.5. $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V,W)$ is an isomorphism if it is an invertible linear map.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0007",
      "sourceStart": 2084,
      "sourceEnd": 2154,
      "sha256": "8b2275f4f5b35010f415d16de658d60bfde9cafcedc09d53bc3de1bb246abd64"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-6",
      "sourceStart": 2154,
      "sourceEnd": 2313,
      "original": "### **1.6**. <span style=\"color: red\">(Quotient Space)</span> Suppose $U$ is a subspace of $V$.  Then $V/U=\\{v+U:v\\in V\\}$ is the set of all translates of $U$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0008",
      "sourceStart": 2313,
      "sourceEnd": 2556,
      "sha256": "1dfaf634c0cd97229de3442f8ebfd26d4d5058a1b75d4bf45ab9fa4a94f960a5"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-7",
      "sourceStart": 2556,
      "sourceEnd": 2665,
      "original": "### 1.7. <span style=\"color: red\">(Quotient Map)</span> Suppose $U$ is a subspace of $V$. Then the linear map",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0009",
      "sourceStart": 2665,
      "sourceEnd": 2741,
      "sha256": "ce83261c21884cfd483570a31b9dfaa58f75184c50bd022a24ad040e9923e46f"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-8",
      "sourceStart": 2741,
      "sourceEnd": 2790,
      "original": "### 1.8. Suppose $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V,W)$. Define",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0010",
      "sourceStart": 2790,
      "sourceEnd": 2977,
      "sha256": "b523d4c01a5a4066c19d8b70c9ee13b3ce181ff0c0e19f3e7f027852044fde44"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-9",
      "sourceStart": 2977,
      "sourceEnd": 3153,
      "original": "### 1.9. <span style=\"color: red\">(Linear Functional)</span> Let $V$ be a vector space. Then the dual space $V'=\\mathcal{L}(V,F)$ is the vector space of all linear functionals.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0011",
      "sourceStart": 3153,
      "sourceEnd": 3155,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-10",
      "sourceStart": 3155,
      "sourceEnd": 3321,
      "original": "### 1.10. <span style=\"color: red\">(Dual Basis)</span> Suppose $\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_n\\}$ is a basis of $V$. Then $\\{\\varphi_1,\\ldots,\\varphi_n\\}$ is called the dual basis.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0012",
      "sourceStart": 3321,
      "sourceEnd": 3569,
      "sha256": "eb9752709a3b88671643aa3735e7d54e93ef0d49e5b513d2e4cec1e1174e565e"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-11",
      "sourceStart": 3569,
      "sourceEnd": 3735,
      "original": "### 1.11. <span style=\"color: red\">(Dual Map)</span> Suppose $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V,W)$. Then the dual map $T'\\in\\mathcal{L}(W',V')$ is defined for each $\\varphi \\in W',$",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0013",
      "sourceStart": 3735,
      "sourceEnd": 3773,
      "sha256": "1bbd904927724b0656f0ab235a9a2003cc7df8e082f7f63fedaca66493643abc"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-1-12",
      "sourceStart": 3773,
      "sourceEnd": 3912,
      "original": "### 1.12. <span style=\"color: red\">(Annihilator)</span> Suppose $U \\subseteq V$. The annihilator of $U$, denoted by $U^0_{V}$ is defined by",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0014",
      "sourceStart": 3912,
      "sourceEnd": 4099,
      "sha256": "8c60ec1022fca42d998c94cf39f49e409c8e7b5185ef558eb7b27da073aaa011"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-section-2",
      "sourceStart": 4099,
      "sourceEnd": 4113,
      "original": "## 2. Theorems",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0015",
      "sourceStart": 4113,
      "sourceEnd": 4115,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-section-2-1",
      "sourceStart": 4115,
      "sourceEnd": 4152,
      "original": "### 2.1. Linear Maps and Isomorphisms",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0016",
      "sourceStart": 4152,
      "sourceEnd": 4154,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-1",
      "sourceStart": 4154,
      "sourceEnd": 4304,
      "original": "#### 2.1.1. Suppose $v_1,\\ldots,v_n$ is a basis of $V$ and $w_1,\\ldots,w_n \\in W$. Then there exists a unique linear map $T:V \\rightarrow W$ such that",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0017",
      "sourceStart": 4304,
      "sourceEnd": 4405,
      "sha256": "81a667935b689c4de2cff40ee5d2e1e687bc8605bda5adc7fadb9bbd741d5c02"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 4405,
      "sourceEnd": 4497,
      "original": "#### 2.1.2. $T\\in\\mathcal{L}(V,W)$ is injective (one-to-one) if and only if $\\ker(T)=\\{0\\}$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0018",
      "sourceStart": 4497,
      "sourceEnd": 4499,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-3",
      "sourceStart": 4499,
      "sourceEnd": 4579,
      "original": "#### 2.1.3. Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional and $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V,W)$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0019",
      "sourceStart": 4579,
      "sourceEnd": 4645,
      "sha256": "4dfbe2dbdcbff2a3abfa24d975219be170c747c51895c0c9d932f115105c71d1"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-4",
      "sourceStart": 4645,
      "sourceEnd": 4703,
      "original": "#### 2.1.4. Let $V,W$ be finite-dimensional vector spaces.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0020",
      "sourceStart": 4703,
      "sourceEnd": 5404,
      "sha256": "8b54971a46de4a2b3888043af6818aa903f8a31e31ca3bec35bacd5caaac5376"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-5",
      "sourceStart": 5404,
      "sourceEnd": 5506,
      "original": "#### 2.1.5. Suppose $S,T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V,W)$. Then $\\mathcal{M}(S+T)=\\mathcal{M}(S)+\\mathcal{M}(T)$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0021",
      "sourceStart": 5506,
      "sourceEnd": 5508,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-6",
      "sourceStart": 5508,
      "sourceEnd": 5680,
      "original": "#### 2.1.6. Suppose $S \\in \\mathcal{L}(V,W)$ and $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(U,V)$ for finite-dimensional vector spaces $U,V,W$. Then $\\mathcal{M}(ST)=\\mathcal{M}(S)\\mathcal{M}(T)$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0022",
      "sourceStart": 5680,
      "sourceEnd": 5682,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-7",
      "sourceStart": 5682,
      "sourceEnd": 5741,
      "original": "#### 2.1.7. Suppose $A \\in F^{m\\times n}, b\\in F^{n}$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0023",
      "sourceStart": 5741,
      "sourceEnd": 5872,
      "sha256": "3bf10045329edc4acac6feada2f9d23ecf10edf205fdac94c76ebf10deeeed8b"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-8",
      "sourceStart": 5872,
      "sourceEnd": 6042,
      "original": "#### 2.1.8. Suppose $A$ is an $m$-by-$n$ matrix with entries in $F$ and column rank $c\\geq1$. Then there exists $C\\in F^{m\\times c}, R\\in F^{c\\times n}$ such that $A=CR$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0024",
      "sourceStart": 6042,
      "sourceEnd": 6044,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-9",
      "sourceStart": 6044,
      "sourceEnd": 6140,
      "original": "#### 2.1.9. Suppose $A\\in F^{m\\times n}$. Then the column rank and row rank of $A$ are the same.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0025",
      "sourceStart": 6140,
      "sourceEnd": 6365,
      "sha256": "46568f90b4644de5a76c23f8585e63424bc812a02ae3b0095737b47afec18516"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-10",
      "sourceStart": 6365,
      "sourceEnd": 6542,
      "original": "#### 2.1.10. Let $V,W$ be finite-dimensional vector spaces with the same dimension, and let $T\\in\\mathcal L(V,W)$. Then either injectivity or surjectivity implies invertibility.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0026",
      "sourceStart": 6542,
      "sourceEnd": 6544,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-11",
      "sourceStart": 6544,
      "sourceEnd": 6691,
      "original": "#### 2.1.11. Suppose $V,W$ are finite-dimensional vector spaces with $\\dim(V)=\\dim(W)$ and let $S\\in \\mathcal{L}(W,V), T\\in \\mathcal{L}(V,W)$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0027",
      "sourceStart": 6691,
      "sourceEnd": 6714,
      "sha256": "d008adad8ea2c855792d58280926b430c242fcd5fdf8d053868d2c4fc1dffac3"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-12",
      "sourceStart": 6714,
      "sourceEnd": 6823,
      "original": "#### 2.1.12. Two finite-dimensional vector spaces are isomorphic if and only if they have the same dimension.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0028",
      "sourceStart": 6823,
      "sourceEnd": 6916,
      "sha256": "a84036c60a9b0eaa6f36ed8185cfbf91cd9b11afe3ff1667eeb2dbc7c286b035"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-13",
      "sourceStart": 6916,
      "sourceEnd": 7175,
      "original": "#### 2.1.13. Let $V,W$ be finite-dimensional vector spaces. Suppose $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(U,V), S \\in \\mathcal{L}(V,W)$, where $\\mathscr{A}=\\{u_1,\\ldots,u_p\\},\\mathscr{B}=\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_n\\},\\mathscr{C}=\\{w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\}$ are bases of $U,V,W$, respectively. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0029",
      "sourceStart": 7175,
      "sourceEnd": 7741,
      "sha256": "84b2578e353d50d1bed0ae2d0033eeb5c14d8ad32084001a069f1e9921dca0e6"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-14",
      "sourceStart": 7741,
      "sourceEnd": 7896,
      "original": "#### 2.1.14. Let $V,W$ be finite-dimensional vector spaces and $T\\in\\mathcal L(V,W)$. Then $\\operatorname{rank}(T)=\\operatorname{colrank}(\\mathcal{M}(T))$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0030",
      "sourceStart": 7896,
      "sourceEnd": 7898,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-1-15",
      "sourceStart": 7898,
      "sourceEnd": 8038,
      "original": "#### 2.1.15. Let $V, W$ be finite-dimensional vector spaces. Let $\\mathscr{A,A'}$ be bases of $V$ and $\\mathscr{B,B'}$ be bases of $W$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0031",
      "sourceStart": 8038,
      "sourceEnd": 8725,
      "sha256": "d8069ad0e3277ee171abb467112c5baf5b75c33b854a445b37e455e20c18f8fc"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-section-2-2",
      "sourceStart": 8725,
      "sourceEnd": 8773,
      "original": "### 2.2. Products and Quotients of Vector Spaces",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0032",
      "sourceStart": 8773,
      "sourceEnd": 8775,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-2-1",
      "sourceStart": 8775,
      "sourceEnd": 8982,
      "original": "#### 2.2.1. Let $V_1,\\ldots,V_m$ be vector spaces over $F$. Then $V=V_1 \\times \\ldots \\times V_m$ is a vector space over $F$. If $V_i$’s are all finite-dimensional, then $\\dim(V)=\\dim(V_1)+\\ldots+\\dim(V_m)$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0033",
      "sourceStart": 8982,
      "sourceEnd": 8984,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-2-2",
      "sourceStart": 8984,
      "sourceEnd": 9166,
      "original": "#### 2.2.2. Suppose $V_1,\\ldots,V_m$ are subspaces of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$. Then $V_1+\\ldots+V_m$ is a direct sum if and only if the following $\\Gamma$ is injective.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0034",
      "sourceStart": 9166,
      "sourceEnd": 9428,
      "sha256": "fa653f0b4b7cfe230f6823d9aa5f5e767424bda5e04fd4faca13fd106c5388f6"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-2-3",
      "sourceStart": 9428,
      "sourceEnd": 9494,
      "original": "#### 2.2.3. Suppose $U$ is a subspace of $V$ and $v,w \\in V$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0035",
      "sourceStart": 9494,
      "sourceEnd": 9650,
      "sha256": "59f7915a57a226de0c17e67be3cb4a2ecf1b0b8062c96ab6333f34a81205afb5"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch03-item-2-2-4",
      "sourceStart": 9650,
      "sourceEnd": 9745,
      "original": "#### 2.2.4. Suppose $V$ is a finite-dimensional vector space and $U$ is a subspace of $V$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch03-block-0036",
